    {"find": "The pattern the exhibit reveals is the central finding of the current-state study. Across the observed inquiries, the median case consumed 5.5 hours of actual work spread over roughly 39 working hours of elapsed time (M). Six hours out of every seven, nobody was working on the inquiry at all. It was waiting \u2014 first to be noticed and assigned, then in the estimator's personal queue behind whatever arrived before it, then, most expensively, on the contractor to answer a clarification, and finally for a discount to be approved by someone in a meeting. The work itself is not slow. The waiting is.",
     "replace": "The exhibit carries the central finding of the current-state study: the median case consumed 5.5 hours of actual work spread over roughly 39 working hours of elapsed time (M). Six hours out of every seven, nobody was working on the inquiry at all \u2014 it was waiting to be assigned, waiting in an estimator's personal queue, waiting on the contractor's answer, waiting for a discount approval. The work is not slow; the waiting is."},
    {"find": "Queue time is not evenly spread. Three steps account for most of it: assignment, where an inquiry waits a median of 14 working hours for an estimator to pick it up; the clarification loop, where the median wait for a contractor's answer is 41 working hours and the process cannot move until it comes; and pricing, where quotes above the discount threshold wait on a manager's availability. Each of these is a handover between people, and each handover is a queue. The cost side is more straightforward. At a fully loaded rate of \u20ac12 per hour \u2014 \u20ac22,000 a year spread over roughly 1,840 productive hours \u2014 applied to both estimation and sales time (E, from payroll and headcount records), the median inquiry carries roughly \u20ac66 of direct labour before any overhead allocation, a figure developed by tier in 4.3.",
     "replace": "Three steps account for most of the queueing: assignment (a median 14 working hours before an estimator picks the inquiry up), the clarification loop (a median 41 working hours for the contractor's answer, during which the process cannot move), and pricing approval. Each is a handover between people, and each handover is a queue. The cost side is simpler: at a fully loaded \u20ac12 per hour \u2014 \u20ac22,000 a year over roughly 1,840 productive hours (E, payroll records) \u2014 the median inquiry carries about \u20ac66 of direct labour, developed by tier in 4.3."},
    {"find": "Treating 640 inquiries as one population would hide more than it reveals, because the inquiry that asks for a single replacement circulator and the inquiry that covers three buildings of a museum have almost nothing in common except the mailbox they arrive in. Every inquiry in the 2025 log was therefore classified retrospectively into one of three tiers using three objective criteria: the number of pumps requested, the completeness of technical data at the moment of receipt, and the project value. Two staff members classified independently; they agreed on 91% of inquiries, and disagreements were resolved jointly (E). The taxonomy carries through the rest of the paper \u2014 the design in Section 7 routes by it, the feasibility audit in Section 8 samples by it, and the financial model in Section 9 builds its benefits by it.",
     "replace": "A single replacement circulator and a three-building museum package have nothing in common except the mailbox they arrive in, so every 2025 inquiry was classified retrospectively into three tiers by pump count, data completeness at receipt, and project value. Two staff members classified independently, agreeing on 91% of inquiries (E). The taxonomy carries through the paper: Section 7 routes by it, Section 8 samples by it, and Section 9 builds its benefits by it."},
    {"find": "The inversion between the last two rows is worth pausing on. Tier 3 supplies one inquiry in eight but nearly half the quoted value, which means the inquiries that matter most commercially are precisely the ones where the manual process is slowest, the documents messiest, and the expertise thinnest \u2014 there are two estimators in the office who can handle a chilled-water package unassisted, and one of them handles most of them. Any redesign that only works for simple inquiries would automate the 12% of value that least needs help.",
     "replace": "The inversion in the last two rows matters: Tier 3 supplies one inquiry in eight but nearly half the quoted value, so the commercially decisive inquiries are exactly where the process is slowest, the documents messiest, and the expertise thinnest \u2014 two estimators can handle a chilled-water package unassisted, and one of them handles most. A redesign that only worked for simple inquiries would automate the 12% of value that least needs help."},
    {"find": "Summed across the year, the direct and traceable consequential cost of the four classes comes to roughly 1.2% of revenue (E). The chain matters as much as the total. Nearly every calculation and selection error in the sample began life as an extraction problem \u2014 a number read off the wrong riser, a scanned schedule transcribed by eye at the end of a long day. Attack the point of entry and much of the downstream taxonomy loses its supply.",
     "replace": "Summed across the year, the traceable cost of the four classes comes to roughly 1.2% of revenue (E). The chain matters as much as the total: nearly every calculation and selection error began life as an extraction problem, so attacking the point of entry starves much of the downstream taxonomy."},
    {"find": "The larger leak, however, never appears in an error report, because every individual instance of it looks like a reasonable commercial decision. Discounting at the distributor is set case by case, by whoever is pricing, without a reference point in front of them. Matching 2025 quotations on scope and value shows discounts on comparable Tier 2 work ranging from 4% to 18% (M) \u2014 not because the jobs differed, but because the days did. Interviews suggest the pattern: under deadline pressure, with the win uncertain and no anchor visible, the safe move is to give a little more away. Estimated against the median discount for like-for-like work, the foregone margin comes to roughly 1.9% of revenue annually (E) \u2014 half again as large as all four error classes combined, and invisible precisely because no single decision was wrong. Together, quality cost and discount leakage run to about 3.1% of revenue, and it is this figure the pricing engine in Section 7.5 is designed against.",
     "replace": "The larger leak never appears in an error report, because each instance looks like a reasonable commercial decision. Discounts are set case by case with no reference point in front of the pricer: matched 2025 quotations show discounts on comparable Tier 2 work ranging from 4% to 18% (M) \u2014 not because the jobs differed, but because the days did. Against the median like-for-like discount, the foregone margin is roughly 1.9% of revenue annually (E), half again as large as all four error classes combined, and invisible precisely because no single decision was wrong. Together, quality cost and discount leakage run to about 3.1% of revenue \u2014 the figure the pricing engine of Section 7.6 is designed against."},
